        <div id="how-we-chose" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. How We Chose These Leg Press Machines</h2>
<p>
            We have not tested these machines. They were chosen from published specifications,
            maker product pages and data sheets, shipping and warranty terms, recall records and
            independent coverage, and the claims on this page are limited to what those sources
            support.
          </p>
          <p>
            We started from design rather than brand, because the design decides most of what a
            leg press is like to own: a 45-degree plate-loaded sled, a leg press/hack squat combo
            built on that sled, a compact sled at a shallower angle, a vertical press, and a
            plate-loaded lever press. For each we looked for models the maker listed as on sale on
            19 October 2026, preferably sold on Amazon US as well. The independent sources were
            Garage Gym Reviews' review of the Titan combo and its coverage of Bells of Steel and
            Force USA combos, Iron Company's listings for the two Body-Solid machines, indexed
            Amazon listings for the Titan combo, Titan's vertical press and the Body-Solid
            GLPH1100, and a UK retailer's listing for the Force USA Compact.
          </p>
          <p>
            Disagreements were common, and they shaped the cards. Titan, Garage Gym Reviews and the
            Amazon title give the Titan combo a 1,000 lb rating, but the Amazon listing's
            specification table gives a maximum weight recommendation of 875 lb. Body-Solid gives
            the GLPH1100B carriage as 60 lb with no plates and Iron Company as 80 lb. Force USA
            has sold several generations of its Compact, and listings for different generations give
            different plate ratings, and Force USA's own page for the current 2027 model gives its footplate as 35 by 29.5
            inches in one place and 30 inches wide in another. Steelflex's data sheet gives the PLLP
            a 12-gauge frame where a dealer lists 11-gauge. In each case we left the figure off the
            specification line and say so in the text.
          </p>
          <p>
            Every brand on this page was checked against the CPSC recall database by recall title,
            manufacturer and recall description on 19 October 2026. The only leg press recall in the
            database is Cybex's December 2012 recall of its model 16110 plate-loaded leg press, a
            commercial machine sold to gyms, worth knowing if you are shopping for used commercial
            equipment. Titan had a 2018 recall alert for weight-lifting safety straps (an accessory
            for its racks, not a leg press). We found no recalls for Body-Solid, Powerline, Bells of
            Steel, Force USA or Steelflex.
          </p>
          <p>
            We deliberately left several machines out. Force USA's Ultimate 45 Degree Leg Press
            Hack Squat Combo ($1,899, with an 85 lb sled and 1,000 lb rating per Force USA) and
            Major Fitness's AH1 Leg Press Hack Squat ($1,399.99, 600 lb weight-post capacity) are
            both on sale, but we could not find independent figures to check the makers' against
            beyond the capacity. RitFit's BLP01 45-degree combo ($799.99) is a budget option we
            could not cross-check either. Mikolo's DD09 combo and LP10 leg sled were out of stock on
            Mikolo's store. Bells of Steel's Iso Leg Press and Hack Squat, which Garage Gym Reviews
            lists at $2,999 and 108 inches long, costs about $1,000 more than the standard version
            for independent footplates. Marcy does not list a standalone leg press; its leg presses
            come as part of stack gyms such as the MWM-999, which belong in our home gym machine
            guide. XMark's current store lists leg extensions and curls but no leg press.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. 45-Degree, Vertical, Lever and Combo Leg Presses</h2>
<p>
            Four designs reach home gyms, and each puts the weight, your body and the floor space
            in a different relationship.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>45-degree sled.</strong> The classic commercial design: you sit or lie on a
            reclined pad and push a plate-loaded sled up rails set at about 45 degrees. It feels
            familiar, suits heavy loading and usually has the most room for your feet, but it is
            long — 84 to 96 inches for the 45-degree machines here — and heavy. Almost every home
            45-degree sled is now sold as a combo with a hack squat.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Leg press/hack squat combo.</strong> The same sled, with a second pad and
            footplate so you can face the other way and squat under shoulder pads. Titan,
            Body-Solid, Bells of Steel and Force USA all switch between modes by flipping or moving
            pads, in seconds to a minute by their accounts. You get two lifts and usually calf
            raises for one footprint, at the cost of some compromise in each: Garage Gym Reviews
            found the Titan's leg press footplate small for some users, and one fixed angle has to
            serve both movements.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Compact shallow-angle sled.</strong> Force USA's Compact runs at 30 degrees in a
            much shorter frame. A shallower angle means the plates feel lighter (see the next
            section), so you load more plates for the same effort, and the movement feels more like
            a squat than a classic press.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Vertical leg press.</strong> You lie on your back under a carriage and press it
            straight up. It is the most compact and cheapest design, and the full weight of the
            plates is on your feet, so you need far fewer plates. The trade-offs are comfort and
            safety: your hips are under the load, the range of motion is set by the stops, and
            getting in and out under a loaded carriage needs care. Many lifters find vertical
            pressing harder on the lower back if the pelvis lifts off the pad, so keep the range
            within what you can do with your back flat.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Lever (pivot) leg press.</strong> The Steelflex PLLP moves the footplate on a
            pivoting arm rather than a sled on rails, with sealed bearings at the pivot. Lever
            machines are common in commercial gyms; the resistance changes slightly through the arc,
            and there are no rails to keep clean. They are usually more expensive and less common in
            home ranges.
          </p>
          <p>
            Leg presses are part of a wider family of lower-body machines. For quad or hamstring
            isolation, see our guide to
            <a href="/best-leg-extension-machines/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">leg extension machines</a>;
            for glute-focused work, the guide to
            <a href="/best-hip-thrust-machines/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">hip thrust machines</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sled-weight" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Sled Weight, Angle and What "1,000 lb" Means</h2>
<p>
            Three numbers on a leg press listing are easy to misread: the starting weight of the
            sled, the angle of the rails, and the rated capacity.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Starting sled weight.</strong> This is what you push before you add a plate. The
            makers give 38 lb for the Force USA Compact, 55 lb for the Bells of Steel, 80 lb for the
            Titan's carriage and 60 lb for the Body-Solid (Iron Company says 80). A light sled
            matters for beginners, for warm-up sets and for single-leg work, and it is why Garage
            Gym Reviews notes that the much heavier Bells of Steel Iso version does not suit anyone
            wanting a light start.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Angle: why the same plates feel different.</strong> On an inclined sled, only
            part of the load acts along the rails. Ignoring friction, the share is the sine of the
            angle: about 71% at 45 degrees, 50% at 30 degrees and 100% on a vertical press. So 270 lb
            of plates on a 55 lb sled at 45 degrees is roughly 230 lb along the rails (325 x 0.71),
            while the same load at 30 degrees is about 163 lb. That is simplified physics from the
            stated angles, not a measurement, and bearings, rollers and the pad angle change the
            feel further, but it explains why leg press numbers do not transfer between machines,
            and why a 400 lb vertical press is not a weak machine: 400 lb straight up is about as
            much as 565 lb on a 45-degree sled.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Design</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Share of load along the path</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">300 lb loaded feels roughly like</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Vertical</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">100%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">300 lb</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">45-degree sled</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 71%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 212 lb</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">30-degree sled</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">50%</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 150 lb</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>Rated capacity.</strong> A "1,000 lb" rating is the most plate the maker says the
            sled or the weight horns will carry. Force USA is unusually explicit: it rates the
            Compact's sled for 700 lb of plates plus a user of up to 320 lb on top. Most makers do
            not say whether body weight is included, so ask if you are heavy or plan to load near
            the limit. The rating is also limited in practice by plate storage and horn length: a
            1,000 lb sled needs about 22 standard 45 lb plates, which few home gyms own, and the
            horns must physically hold them. Treat the rating as headroom, not a target.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Rails, rollers and bearings.</strong> Sleds run on linear ball bearings on
            steel shafts (Bells of Steel), on wheels or rollers on a track (Titan's urethane
            wheels per Garage Gym Reviews, Body-Solid's quad-track rollers) or on bushings (the
            Powerline's nylon bushings). Linear bearings are usually the smoothest; rollers and
            bushings are cheaper and still smooth if kept clean and lubricated as the manual says.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Safety Catches, Lockouts and Loading</h2>
<p>
            A leg press feels safe because your back is supported, but a loaded sled is a few
            hundred pounds of steel and iron moving toward your chest. The safety features differ
            more than the listings suggest.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Starting lockouts.</strong> The handles beside the seat on a 45-degree
            sled release and re-engage the sled at its starting position. Titan and Body-Solid
            give three lockout positions, Bells of Steel five racking positions and Force USA eight
            on the Compact. More positions let people of different heights start from a comfortable
            depth.</li>
            <li><strong>Bottom stops.</strong> Separate from the lockouts, these set a hard limit on
            how far the sled can travel toward you. Force USA describes safety stops built into
            the Compact's travel path; the Steelflex has an adjustable stop system that allows half
            or full range, per Steelflex. Set the stop just below the deepest point you can reach
            with your lower back flat on the pad, so a failed rep ends on the stop rather than on
            your knees.</li>
            <li><strong>Vertical presses.</strong> On a vertical machine the stops are your only
            protection, because there is nowhere for the carriage to go except down. Set them every
            session and never unlock the carriage until your feet are flat on the plate.</li>
            <li><strong>Loading order.</strong> Load and unload evenly, a plate on each side at a
            time where there are two horns, so the sled does not twist on its rails. Take plates off
            after training. Garage Gym Reviews suggests leaving the Titan's sled lowered when not in
            use, so a child cannot release it.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Technique matters as much as hardware. Keep your lower back and hips on the pad
            throughout — if your pelvis curls up at the bottom, you have gone deeper than the
            machine and your hips allow — keep your knees tracking over your feet, and do not lock
            your knees out hard under heavy load. Do not hold your breath through long sets; a hard
            leg press raises blood pressure sharply, which is one reason anyone with high blood
            pressure or a heart condition should get advice first. Our guide to
            <a href="/best-recovery-tools-for-weightlifters/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">recovery tools for weightlifters</a>
            covers what helps after a heavy leg session.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Footprint, Plate Storage and Floor Loading</h2>
<p>
            Leg presses are long, and a loaded one is the heaviest single thing many home gyms
            hold. These are the makers' assembled figures, rounded.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead><tr>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Machine</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Length x width</th>
              <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Height</th>
            </tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Body-Solid GLPH1100B</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">96 x 34 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">56 in.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Bells of Steel combo</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">87 x 37.5 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">58 in.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Titan combo</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">84 x 40 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">53 in.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Steelflex PLLP</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">84 x 53 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">56 in.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Force USA Compact</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">65 x 52 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">57 in.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Titan vertical press</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">48 x 38 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">52.5 in. at full extension</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Powerline PVLP156X</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">45 x 47 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">55 in.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>Working room.</strong> You get on and off a 45-degree machine from the side,
            load plates from the side or under the sled, and switch pads on a combo from the end.
            Force USA's manual recommends 3 to 5 feet of clearance around the Compact, which turns a
            52 by 65 inch footprint into a planning area of roughly 10 by 11 feet. That is a sensible
            rule for any of these machines: mark the frame and three feet around it on the floor
            before you order. Ceiling height is rarely an issue — none stands taller than 58 inches —
            but low doorways and basement stairs can be, so check that the longest carton and the
            assembled frame can reach the room.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Plate storage.</strong> Most combos store plates on horns on the frame or, on
            the Bells of Steel, under the sled. On-frame storage keeps the floor clear but makes the
            machine heavier on its feet and harder to move; a separate plate tree spreads the load.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Floor loading.</strong> On a ground-floor slab this is rarely a concern, but a
            loaded 45-degree press upstairs deserves arithmetic. Titan, Garage Gym Reviews and the
            Amazon listing agree that the Titan combo weighs 325 lb in an 84 by 40 inch footprint,
            about 23 square feet. Add 450 lb of plates on and around it and a 200 lb lifter, and the
            total is about 975 lb, or roughly 42 lb per square foot averaged over the frame. That is
            our arithmetic from those inputs, not a measurement — and it is already above the 40 lb
            per square foot that the International Residential Code uses as the design live load
            for living areas (30 for sleeping rooms), before you allow for the load sitting on the
            frame's feet. As general guidance rather than engineering advice: put a 45-degree leg
            press on the ground floor or a slab if you can; upstairs, choose a vertical press or ask
            a structural engineer first, and keep plate storage elsewhere. Use rubber flooring
            either way; our
            <a href="/best-gym-flooring/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">gym flooring guide</a>
            compares mats and tiles.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Delivery and assembly.</strong> Body-Solid ships the GLPH1100B in five boxes
            totalling 340 lb; Bells of Steel ships its combo in six boxes and says to plan two to
            four hours with a second person; Force USA says most people build the Compact in about 90
            minutes and ships heavy items by freight to the curb. We have not assembled these
            machines. The habits that prevent most problems are the same as for any heavy machine,
            and our guide to
            <a href="/how-to-assemble-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">assembling a treadmill</a>
            covers unboxing, tools and two-person lifting.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fit" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Fit: Your Height, Footplate Size and Range of Motion</h2>
<p>
            A leg press that does not fit your body will either stop you short of a useful depth
            or push you past a safe one. Check these before you buy, especially if you are well
            above or below average height.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Height range.</strong> Force USA gives the Compact a recommended user height
            of 5 ft 0 in to 6 ft 8 in. Bells of Steel says its combo has worked for lifters from 5 ft
            0 in to 6 ft 2 in and 265 lb. Garage Gym Reviews thought the Titan combo's hack squat
            would not suit people 6 ft 3 in or taller. Most other makers do not publish a range.</li>
            <li><strong>Footplate size.</strong> Titan gives its leg press footplate as 21 by 15
            inches, which Garage Gym Reviews found narrow for wide stances; Bells of Steel's is 26
            by 13.75 inches. A bigger plate lets you move your feet higher for more glute and
            hamstring work, or lower for more quad, and suits broad lifters.</li>
            <li><strong>Back pad and seat.</strong> An adjustable back pad lets you start deeper or
            shallower. On a vertical press, the length of the back pad and the four starting heights
            Titan gives its vertical press decide how close the footplate sits to you.</li>
            <li><strong>User weight limits.</strong> Steelflex's data sheet gives the PLLP a maximum
            user weight of 120 kg, about 265 lb, and Force USA gives the Compact 320 lb. If you are
            heavier, ask the maker before buying, whatever the plate rating.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Range of motion is set by your hips and lower back, not by the machine. The deepest
            position worth using is the one where your lower back stays flat on the pad. If you are
            unsure what range suits your knees or hips, a physiotherapist or qualified coach can
            set it with you.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Who Should Buy Something Else</h2>
<p>
            A leg press is a single-purpose machine with a large footprint. Several readers will
            get more from the same money or space elsewhere.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Anyone without a squat rack yet.</strong> A
            <a href="/best-squat-racks-for-home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">squat rack</a>
            and barbell train the legs and the whole body for less floor space. Most coaches would
            put a rack before a leg press in a first home gym.</li>
            <li><strong>Lifters who already own a rack and want a guided squat.</strong> A
            <a href="/best-smith-machines/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Smith machine</a>
            or rack attachment adds a guided bar without another long frame; a
            <a href="/best-safety-squat-bars/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">safety squat bar</a>
            is easier on the shoulders.</li>
            <li><strong>People who want leg work and upper-body stations together.</strong> A
            stack <a href="/best-home-gym-machines/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home gym</a>
            with a leg press attachment, or a
            <a href="/best-cable-machines-for-home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">cable machine</a>,
            does more per square foot, though with less leg loading.</li>
            <li><strong>Upstairs rooms and small budgets.</strong> A vertical press, or
            <a href="/best-adjustable-dumbbells/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">adjustable dumbbells</a>
            for split squats and lunges, is lighter on the floor and the wallet.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            If you are still planning the room, our
            <a href="/home-workout-equipment-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">home workout equipment reviews</a>
            put the leg press alongside the other big purchases, and the
            <a href="/home-gym/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Home Gym hub</a>
            lists every guide in this section.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best leg press machine for a home gym?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people with the floor space, a 45-degree leg press and hack squat combo such as the Titan Fitness Leg Press Hack Squat Machine, whose size, weight and angle are confirmed by Titan, a hands-on review and an Amazon listing. For a small room or tight budget, a vertical press such as the Body-Solid Powerline PVLP156X costs about a third as much.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a vertical or 45-degree leg press better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 45-degree press is more comfortable for most people, has more room for your feet and suits heavy loading, but it is seven to eight feet long. A vertical press is compact and cheap and puts the whole load on your feet, so you need fewer plates, but getting under the carriage needs care and your lower back must stay flat. Set its stops every session.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a leg press sled weigh?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It varies by machine. The makers on this page give 38 lb for the Force USA Compact, 55 lb for the Bells of Steel, 80 lb for the Titan combo and 60 lb for the Body-Solid GLPH1100B, which Iron Company lists at 80 lb. Add the sled weight to your plates when you log a set.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why does the same weight feel lighter on a leg press than on a squat?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On an inclined sled only part of the load acts along the rails. Ignoring friction, about 71% acts along a 45-degree sled and 50% along a 30-degree one, while a vertical press takes all of it. Your back is also supported, so you are not stabilising your trunk. That is why leg press numbers do not transfer between machines or to squats.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much space does a home leg press need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The 45-degree combos on this page are 84 to 96 inches long and 34 to 40 inches wide, the Force USA Compact is 65 by 52 inches, and vertical presses fit in about 4 by 4 feet. Allow about three feet around the frame for getting on, loading plates and switching pads; Force USA's manual recommends 3 to 5 feet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I put a leg press upstairs?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A vertical press, usually; a loaded 45-degree press needs care. By our arithmetic from Titan's figures, a 325 lb combo with 450 lb of plates and a 200 lb lifter averages about 42 lb per square foot over its footprint, above the 40 lb residential design live load for living areas. Prefer a slab, or ask a structural engineer. This is general guidance, not engineering advice.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Leg Press</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Each of these turns a large, heavy machine into one that disappoints, does not fit or is less safe than it should be.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing plate totals across different angles</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">300 lb on a vertical press, a 45-degree sled and a 30-degree sled are three different efforts. Compare machines of the same design, and judge progress against your own sessions on your own machine.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying for the frame size, not the room size</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">You get on from the side, load plates from the side or under the sled and switch pads from the end. Mark the frame plus about three feet around it on the floor before ordering, and check the delivery route for the longest carton.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the plate size the machine takes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Most leg presses take 2-inch Olympic plates, but the Powerline PVLP156X has 1-inch posts for standard plates. Check the post diameter against the plates you own, and budget for enough plates to make the machine useful.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving the stops too low, or not using them</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Bottom stops only protect you if they sit just below the deepest point you can reach with your lower back flat on the pad. Set them every session, especially on a vertical press, and lower or unload the sled when you finish.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

